The temperature at which a typical 40% alcohol vodka freezes is the same as that of most other alcoholic beverages. This is due to the principle of freezing point depression, which occurs when a solute is added to a solvent. The more the solute (in this case, ethanol from the alcohol), the lower the freezing point of the solution.

For a typical 40% alcohol vodka, the freezing point is approximately -18°C. This is lower than 0°C. Therefore, the comparison is that it is lower than 0 degrees Celsius, with an estimate of -18°C.

